GlitchShifter: reset the delay taps when the width changes

The buffer width comes from Tighten: 65536 samples at 0, 6 at 1. When it
changed, only crossesL/R and realzeroesL/R were reset. positionL/R,
sincezerocrossL/R and the zero-cross registry (offsetL/R[]...) kept values
made with the old width. A splice then jumped back to such a stale offset,
and the tap crawled back by `width` per sample, while the
`while (count > width) count -= width` loops cost (distance / width)
iterations per sample. Raising Tighten live from 0 to 1 gave blocks of up
to 108 % of a 128-frame period at 48 kHz on a 5 GHz x86_64, for as long
as the setting stayed.

- On a width change, also clear the registry, the tap positions and the
  zero-cross counters, as activate() does (the audio buffer is kept), and
  smooth the jump of a moving tap like a splice does.
- Replace the wrap loops with an exact constant-time equivalent.

With fixed settings, the output is bit-identical to the unpatched build.

--- a/src/GlitchShifter/GlitchShifter.c
+++ b/src/GlitchShifter/GlitchShifter.c
@@ -180,6 +180,20 @@
 	while (glitchShifter->fpdR < 16386) glitchShifter->fpdR = rand() * UINT32_MAX;
 }
 
+// Same result as the "while (c < 0) c += width; while (c > width) c -= width;"
+// pairs it replaces, in constant time: those loops ran once per sample and
+// cost (distance / width) iterations, which is huge after width shrinks.
+static inline int wrapCount(int c, int width)
+{
+	if (c < 0) {
+		c %= width;
+		if (c < 0) c += width;
+	} else if (c > width) {
+		c = (c - 1) % width + 1;
+	}
+	return c;
+}
